@@ -89,6 +89,8 @@ The mirror term IV uses the fourth-order central difference in $v_\parallel$ wit
 ([Candy et al. 2016](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcp.2016.07.039)): it removes from
 the dissipated distribution the components that source the field equations, which suppresses a low-$k_y$ numerical instability of electromagnetic runs reported for GKW (GKW issue 201).
 
+`disp_par_neutral` (off by default, not in GKW, single device; the CUDA backend computes the term in JAX) is a second variant. With $D$ the plain parallel dissipation, $u = (Z/T) J_0 F_M$ and $\rho[x]$ the gyro-averaged charge of $x$ summed over species, $f$ is split into its charge-carrying part $P f = u\,\rho[f]/\rho[u]$ and the rest, and the dissipation becomes $D f - P D (f - P f)$. The charge made by dissipating the charge-free part of $f$ is removed, so that part does no work on the potential; the charge-carrying part is dissipated as before. The `disp_par_neutral_end` points (default 2) next to an open end of a $k_x$ chain keep the plain dissipation, which is the boundary condition there. The option costs one more parallel stencil per right-hand side.
+
 ## Nonlinear term
 
 Term III is pseudospectral, as in GKW `non_linear_terms.F90`. The derivatives $ik_x\hat\chi$, $ik_y\hat\chi$, $ik_x\hat g$, $ik_y\hat g$ are transformed to a zero-padded real-space grid of `mrad` x `mphi` points (3/2 rule), multiplied and transformed back, and only the retained modes are kept. The FFT lengths, the packing of two derivatives into one transform and the FP32 option are described in {doc}`../performance/index`. They change the result only at round-off.
